 obscure literary and  historical references scattered  throughout the lego movie the fantasy  realm is named middle zealand a  combination of middle-earth the setting  of the Lord of the Rings books and New  Zealand where Peter Jackson's Lord of  the Rings movies were filmed  meanwhile clouds  cuckoo-land princess unikitty is bright  and shiny homeland in the sky is a  direct literary reference the idea of an  impossibly perfect city in the clouds of  the same name first shows up all the way  back in 414 BC in the birds a play  written by Greek playwright Aristophanes  and then there's a wide sage Vitruvius  he's named after a first century BC  Roman philosopher best known for writing  a 10 volume series of books about  architecture fitting for a character and  a flick made of bricks honey how did I  meet your mother the most popular TV  show in the lego verse is a dumb one  joke affair called where are my pants  where a pantsless character frequently  wanders aloud about the whereabouts of  his fans honey  where am I hey kids may miss it but  adults recognize it's a jab at sitcom  that's still air long after their once  fresh premises have grown stale sitcoms  like say How I Met Your Mother which  took 8 seasons to introduce the audience  to The Namesake mother and also happened  to feature the Lego movies Phil Lord and  Christopher Miller as writers during his  first season inside jokes there's a  scene where wyldstyle names a bunch of  the realms of the lego universe and each  gets a second or two on screen your home  Richard is one of many realms in the  universe there's also this one Pirate's  Cove a striking Blanding clown town a  bunch of others we don't need to mention  savvy older viewers know why because  they're discontinued or less popular  lego sets such as the controversial for  girls friends line a set based on the  big-screen bomb of Speed Racer the Syfy  Bionicle line which disappeared from  stores in 2010 and the despised fabula  and line from the 1980s mixed signals  it's not every day that a cheery comedy  movie made for kids is also a subversive  satire a critical read of the lego movie  shows that it's quite anti corporate and  anti-capitalist  the villain of the movie is an evil  money and power-hungry tycoon and tyrant  named president business known later as  Lord business  shortly after the lego movies released  in 2014  figures from the political left such as  documentary filmmaker Michael Moore  praised  films anti-business cinema while more  conservative entities such as the fox  news channel decried it you know listen  Hollywood has its own agenda and we're  kind of used to this but it feels it  feels a little bit more threatening when  they start to push this out to our kids  of course can a movie that also serves  as a highly effective feature-length  commercial for a toy line really be that  anti capitalism pure imagination at the  end of the movie it's revealed that the  action up to that point took place  within the imagination of a Lego loving  little boy playing with a massive Lego  collection owned by his father played by  Will Ferrell who also provides the voice  of Lord business so all of that world  building world mixing and searching for  the piece of resistance didn't actually  happen it was all just a figment of the  kid's imagination and a neat little  parable about creativity trust and  believing in yourself but by the time  the sequel teasing ending rolls around  most kids have already forgotten all  that if they got the message in the  first place and from no fair Joe Klein  we are here to destroy you  oh man thanks for watching click the  looper icon to subscribe to our YouTube  channel let's check out all this cool  stuff we know you'll love to  [Music]
 

